          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caballus, caballī — \"a nag, a pack horse\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Caballus vetus et lentus est, sed sarcinās portat. The nag is old and slow, but it carries the bags."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, masculine: caballus, caballī m. An everyday, low word; the fine word is equus. French cheval and English cavalry come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caballus is usually traced to Gaulish caballos 'pack horse' (EtymDB); Lewis & Short also call it perhaps Celtic and compare Greek kaballēs. French cheval and, through Italian, English cavalry go back to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
