          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: onerō, onerāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "onerō, onerāre — \"to load, to burden\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Servī mūlōs sarcinīs onerant. The slaves load the mules with baggage."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "onerō, onerāre, onerāvī, onerātum, with the thing in the ablative. English onerous and exonerate come from the same root."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "onerō is built on onus, oneris 'a load, a burden', from PIE h₃énh₂os (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English onerous and exonerate come from the same family."
            }
          ]
        },
